<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<sdf version="1.7">
  <world name="closed_loop_track3">
    <include>
      <uri>model://sun</uri>
    </include>
    <include>
      <uri>model://ground_plane</uri>
    </include>

    <!-- User-built world (Gazebo Building Editor), saved at
         /home/divagar/building_editor_models/track3. Requires
         GAZEBO_MODEL_PATH to include /home/divagar/building_editor_models
         (already set in buggy_description's gazebo.launch.py).

         Much larger and more complex than rect_loop_track.world /
         varying_width_track.world (~111m x 174m overall): a large open
         outer area plus a smaller interconnected room/corridor cluster
         roughly in x=[-55,-18], y=[-88,-10]. Measured via a rasterized
         clearance map (walls -> occupancy grid -> distance transform),
         not eyeballed:
           - The room cluster is a genuine mix of widths - about 12% of
             its free-space cells are narrower than 2m (a real, too-tight-
             for-this-vehicle doorway/passage, the same class of problem
             that made closed_loop_track2.world/track1 unusable for this
             vehicle's 4.4m turning radius), and about 31% are 5.6m+
             (comfortably navigable).
           - Default spawn point below sits in a clean, fully-enclosed
             9.6m-wide corridor between two long parallel walls (Wall_43/
             Wall_50 in track3/model.sdf) - verified wide, not guessed.
         Autonomous driving may still wander into one of the narrow
         sub-2m spots elsewhere in the cluster - that's an expected,
         known limitation of this specific world, not a planner bug (see
         the same finding on closed_loop_track2.world earlier this
         session). -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://track3</uri>
      <name>closed_loop_track3_walls</name>
      <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
    </include>

    <physics type="ode">
      <real_time_update_rate>1000.0</real_time_update_rate>
      <max_step_size>0.001</max_step_size>
    </physics>
  </world>
</sdf>
